Digitization for TB2026-06
==========================

Input data:

  /home/llr/ilc/shi/data/SiWECAL-Prototype/TB2026-06/CONF6/

Generation code:

  /home/llr/ilc/shi/code/Energy-Reco/Generation/generation/run_scripts/TB2026-06

MuonCalib.ipynb is used to get the MIP value.  For CONF6 with 0.5 mm Si, the
current value is:

  0.1472 MeV/MIP


Cell shaping
------------

The common C++ implementation is in:

  include/CellShaping.hh

It is header-only and provides:

  siwecal::shapeCellSteps(...)

`shapeCellSteps` returns the digitized result with the original
`CellShapingResult` output fields.

The fast-version point finder is separated into:

  include/Shaping_FastVersion.hh

It provides:

  siwecal::shapingFastVersion(...)

Here `FastVersion` means the faster calculation version.  It is not the
electronics fast shaper channel.  `shapingFastVersion` still returns both fast
and slow shaping key points: fast peak, slow peak, trigger point, and slow
sample point.  It does not build or store the full shaping curve.

Current default configuration:

  mipValueGeV   = 0.0001472
  mipThreshold  = 0.5
  delayNs       = 160.0
  tauFastNs     = 30.0
  tauSlowNs     = 180.0
  orderFast     = 2
  orderSlow     = 2
  fastWindowNs  = 200.0
  slowWindowNs  = 500.0
  fastNoiseMIP  = 1/30
  slowNoiseMIP  = 1/12
  peakSearchBins    = 64
  refineIterations  = 48
  triggerSearchBins = 64

Noise is added in MIP units to the reported fast peak, slow peak, and slow
sample amplitudes.  Peak times are found with a coarse bracket plus golden
section refinement.  The trigger time is found by bracketing the first fast
threshold crossing and refining it with bisection.  The slow sample point is
computed directly from the slow shaping function at `trigger_time + delayNs`,
then one additional slow-noise sample is added.

Before any peak or trigger search, both `shapeCellSteps` and
`shapingFastVersion` check the raw hit energy:

  sum(stepEnergyGeV) / mipValueGeV < mipThreshold

If this is true, the function returns the default zero/invalid output directly
and skips the shaping calculation.


Build
-----

Build the C++ test executable:

  cmake -S . -B build
  cmake --build build

Run it:

  ./build/test_cell_shaping


Python module for notebooks
---------------------------

`CMakeLists.txt` can also build a pybind11 module:

  cell_shaping_cpp

Use the TB2026/key4hep notebook environment so CMake uses the same Python as the
`root_torch_tb2026` kernel:

  source /home/llr/ilc/shi/code/SiWECAL-Prototype/generation/run_scripts/TB2026-06/env.sh
  PYEXE=$(python -c "import sys; print(sys.executable)")
  cmake -S . -B build_py313 -DPython3_EXECUTABLE="$PYEXE"
  cmake --build build_py313

The module is written to:

  build_py313/python/cell_shaping_cpp*.so

The Python module exposes `shape_cell_steps(...)` and
`shaping_fast_version(...)`.
